---
// Validation notes, summarized from statjump-app/analysis/out/validation.txt and
// statjump-app/docs/validation-2026-10-12.md (analyzer 0.8.0 to 0.13.0, rubric 0.2).
// Numbers are converted to inches. Update this page when new validation rounds land.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const updated = 'October 16, 2026';
---
<Base title="Validation" description="How Stat Jump's measurements are being tested, what we found, and what's still in progress.">
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Validation</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated {updated}. Further testing is in progress.</p>

    <p>
      Stat Jump measures your jump from a phone video. This page explains how we're checking those numbers, what
      we've found so far, and what still needs work. These are early results from our own testing, not lab
      measurements.
    </p>

    <h2>What we tested</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>20 jumps by one athlete</strong> (6'2"), filmed on an iPhone on a tripod at head height, from 10 and
        15 feet away, in portrait and landscape. 15 were filmed side-on and 5 at an angle of about 45°.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reference marks:</strong> tape on a wall at 9'0" that the athlete reached for on every jump, and a
        tape measure along the floor.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Two earlier clips</strong> from other players of known height, used as sanity checks.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we found</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Every side-on jump was measured:</strong> 15 of 15.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vertical:</strong> each jump is measured two independent ways, from time in the air and from how far
        the hips rise. Across the side-on jumps the two agreed to within 1.6 inches on average. The verticals
        averaged 18.8 inches and stayed consistent from jump to jump (a spread of 1.3 inches).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Take-off and landing timing</strong> matched frames picked out by hand on the jumps we checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Touch height</strong> averaged within a fraction of an inch of the 9'0" tape. That result comes
        after a correction tuned on these same jumps, so it needs a second athlete to confirm it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Footwork:</strong> on all 15 side-on jumps it correctly identified the penultimate step, the
        closing step and both feet in the plant.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slow motion:</strong> given a slow-motion clip, it refused to treat it as real time instead of
        reporting an impossible jump.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Phone setup:</strong> portrait and landscape gave the same results, and so did 10 and 15 feet away
        (15 feet was the most consistent). At 30fps the numbers held up, but the uncertainty doubled to about ±4
        inches and ground contact time wasn't scored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Angled filming:</strong> the vertical was measured on 4 of the 5 angled jumps, in line with side-on.
        Past about 40° off side-on, knee and trunk angles are left unmeasured rather than reported wrong.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not validated yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ground contact time, knee bend, arm swing and step lengths have no reference measurements yet.</li>
      <li>All of the main testing used one athlete. The results need more players, body types and gyms.</li>
      <li>The reference was a wall tape, not a Vertec or a jump mat.</li>
      <li>
        The standing-reach estimate used when you skip that question ran about 4 inches high for one athlete. We're
        collecting more data before changing it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>In progress</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Measured verticals (Vertec or jump mat) and measured standing reach for every test clip.</li>
      <li>More athletes, across levels, heights and gyms.</li>
      <li>A camera-angle sweep at 0°, 15°, 30° and 45° to firm up the angle limits.</li>
      <li>Hand-marked frames to check ground contact timing and step lengths.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><a href="/">Back to Stat Jump</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
